proposals.what:
The builder tree gets a Seg above it: Skills · Question types · Traps.

The Traps view lists the student's misconceptions grouped by norm. Each row has:
- the label;
- a status chip (Open / Half fixed / Relapsed / Check-in due / Fixed);
- hits and last seen;
- 'lives in 4 question types';
- a checkbox.

A second block, 'Common traps in these skills', shows the 10 norms with the highest share of all learners' wrong first tries, with n printed. It serves students with no history.

Choosing traps sets pool 'related' with ids = the norms. Every served question is trap-loaded:
- want across every template whose options can carry that norm;
- progress.related() covers 79 cross-template norms today, and the rest are template-specific.

Presets:
- 'My top traps · 5' (hits x recency);
- 'Traps most students fall for'.

In the session, the why-line names the trap in play, and the verdict says Resisted or Fell for it.

The summary gets a 'By trap' table: trap · on the table 4x · resisted 3 · status 'Open -> Half fixed' · [Again].

Templates gain an optional desmos(item) that returns LaTeX expressions (and an optional table or regression) built from item.params:
- systems: both lines;
- nonlinear equations: y = f(x) and y = k;
- circles: the circle equation;
- two-variable data: the table plus y1 ~ m x1 + b;
- function values: f(x) and x = value.

After answering (or as hint 2 in learning mode, filed as assisted), a 'Solve it with Desmos' button opens the docked calculator with the expressions loaded via setExpression, with one caption such as 'The intersection is the solution'.

Other surfaces:
- A 'Desmos-friendly' strip chip on types with a one-graph solution.
- A builder checkbox 'Desmos-friendly types only' and a 'Desmos strategies' preset.
- Summary: 'Used the Desmos way 3 · by hand 7'.

A coverage script prints '<n> of 386 types have a Desmos way', and coverage grows template by template, as spr_mistakes does.

The share sheet (R18 sets) gets a Seg: 'Everyone gets their own numbers (twins)', the default, or 'Everyone gets these exact questions'.

With twins, each taker gets the same templates, tier, format and order on their own seeds. Each slot is trap-locked to the same misconception (Proposal 1), so answers can't be passed around but slots stay comparable.

The sharer's results sheet:
- per slot: 'Q3 · Count solutions · Medium · 5/7 right · fell for: divided only the constant 2, sign slip 1' (UI.spread by misconception, not by letter);
- a 'Weakest slots' row;
- takers with score and time;
- 'Compare two takers', slot by slot.

[Reteach weak slots] creates, in one tap, a follow-up twin set of the slots under 60% right and their traps, with its own link. That gives a free homework loop.

The taker's landing says: 'Your numbers are your own; you compare by question type.'

Every item already passes satbank.audit: a separately written solver re-derives the key from item.params before the item is served.

Details gains:
- 'Verified · a second solver, written separately, gets 2/3 from the same numbers';
- [Show the check], which lists the named params ('slope 3, intercept −2, input 4 -> 10').

In the Report sheet, choosing 'Wrong answer key' or 'More than one answer is right' runs a server check before Send:
- regenerate (template, seed);
- audit it;
- confirm that no other option's value equals the key.

The result shows at once: 'Checked just now: the key holds. Reports like this usually come from <the top trap on this question>', with [Send anyway].

If the check fails (a rendering fault, or two equal values):
- the report is filed as urgent;
- that (template, seed) is blocked from serving for everyone;
- the student's attempt is marked disputed, so it isn't counted.

parity_gaps.upgrade:
Make each label act:
- a 'your trap · 3x' tag on options matching your history;
- 'Practise this trap' on each label (5 trap-locked twins across types);
- the crowd share per option by misconception (UI.spread).
The label becomes a way into study, not just a caption.
